  "subtitle": "A handheld electronic field book that a surveyor wears on a strap and cables into a telescope that reads the world",
  "why_scout_cares": "The REC-100 (Deutsches Museum 'Rec Elta 3') is the museum's cleanest embodied field-computing artifact: a little calculator-keypad recorder the surveyor wears on a shoulder strap and plugs into a Zeiss Elta 3 total station, so every measured angle and distance streams into a handheld microcomputer while the operator crouches over a tripod in the field. The 'computer' is a physical pendant that hands numbers from a wheel-and-lens measuring machine to an office box at the end of the day. It fills a wholly unrepresented category — embodied surveying/field-computing HCI — and pairs naturally with the Yeoman Navigator (paper chart, hand-guided) and the plotted-output family. Nothing like it is in the collection.",
